[' Here is a simple C program that does what you asked for. This program takes one command-line argument, converts it from string to integer, adds 1000 to the result, and then prints the result.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Check if a command-line argument was provided
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s number\n", argv[0]);
        return -1;
    }

    // Convert the command-line argument from string to integer
    int num = atoi(argv[1]);

    // Add 1000 to the number and print the result
    printf("%d + 1000 = %d\n", num, num + 1000);

    return 0;
}
```

To run this program, save it as a .c file (for example, `add_1000.c`) and compile it using gcc:

```sh
gcc add_1000.c -o add_1000
```

Then you can run the compiled program with any number as an argument:

```sh
./add_1000 5
```

The output will be:

```
5 + 1000 = 1005
```']

