['Here is a simple C code that does what you've described. This program uses the standard library function `argc` and `argv` for command-line arguments, and `strlen` and `memmove` to handle trimming of trailing whitespace.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc <= 1) {
        printf("Usage: %s argument\n", argv[0]);
        return -1;
    }

    // Find the index of the last non-whitespace character in the argument.
    int arg_len = strlen(argv[1]);
    for (int i = arg_len - 1; i >= 0 && isspace(argv[1][i]); --i) {}

    // Trim trailing whitespace from the argument.
    int new_arg_len = arg_len - (arg_len - i);
    argv[1][new_arg_len] = '\0';
    memmove(argv[1], argv[1] + i, new_arg_len + 1);

    // Print the trimmed argument to the console.
    printf("Trimmed argument: %s\n", argv[1]);

    return 0;
}
```

You can compile this code with GCC using the command `gcc -o program_name program_name.c`, and then run it using the command `./program_name argument`. Replace `program_name` with the name you want for your executable file.']

