['Here's a simple Flask application that fulfills your requirements. I've used SQLite3 for database management and Flask-SQLAlchemy for easier database operations.

```python
from flask import Flask, request, redirect, url_for
from flask_sqlalchemy import SQLAlchemy

app = Flask(__name__)
app.config['SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI'] = 'sqlite:////tmp/test.db'  # Database URI
db = SQLAlchemy(app)

class UserMessage(db.Model):
    id = db.Column(db.Integer, primary_key=True)
    username = db.Column(db.String(80), nullable=False)
    message = db.Column(db.Text, nullable=False)

@app.route('/post', methods=['POST'])
def post():
    username = request.form.get('username')
    message = request.form.get('message')

    if not username or not message:
        return "Invalid input", 400

    user_message = UserMessage(username=username, message=message)
    db.session.add(user_message)
    db.session.commit()

    return redirect(url_for('main'))

@app.route('/')
def main():
    messages = UserMessage.query.order_by(UserMessage.id.desc()).all()
    return '<h1>Messages:</h1><br/>'.join([f"{message.username}: {message.message}" for message in messages])

if __name__ == '__main__':
    app.run(debug=True)
```

This code creates a Flask application with two routes: one for handling the submission of messages (`/post`) and another for displaying the messages on the main page (`/`). The `post()` function gets the username and message from the request, checks their validity, inserts them into the database using the `insert_user_message_in_db()` function (which is not explicitly defined here but can be a regular SQL query), and then redirects the user to the main page. The messages are displayed on the main page with their respective users by iterating through the records in the database.

To run this code, save it as a .py file and execute it using Python: `python app.py`. By default, it will listen on port 5000. Access the application at http://127.0.0.1:5000/. You can submit messages by sending POST requests to http://127.0.0.1:5000/post with username and message parameters.']
